// Darwin 1859, On the Origin of Species, p. 168 - the passage that names natural selection.
// The transcription travels with a note saying that neither the wording nor the pinpoint has
// been checked against the volume, and that the two ellipsis marks are the author's own
// elisions. Both facts are recorded here rather than left on disk.
//
// FORM. A chain of conditionals whose antecedents Darwin asserts as he states them - 'and I
// think this cannot be disputed', 'and this certainly cannot be disputed' - so the chain
// detaches. It detaches at every link but one, and that one is marked.
//
//   <Useful variations must have occurred>        argument from analogy with the domestic
//                                                 case, and from what it would be
//                                                 extraordinary to find absent
//   <Preservation of favourable variations>       the principle itself
//   <Diversification pays>                        Darwin's 'Therefore', which reaches back
//                                                 across an elision
//   <Varieties grow into species>                 Darwin's 'Thus': the divergence conclusion
//   <The excerpt omits the ground of divergence>  the reconstruction's own undercut
//
// Two apex claims, because the passage ends twice: with the divergence conclusion, and with
// the naming of the principle. Neither rests on the other.

