// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The claims
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

[Organic beings vary]: During the long course of ages and under varying conditions of life, organic beings vary at all in the several parts of their organisation.
    {
    fidelity: "quotation"
    source: "\"during the long course of ages and under varying conditions of life, organic beings vary at all in the several parts of their organisation\""
    note: "Darwin gives this as the antecedent of a conditional and endorses it in the same breath - 'and I think this cannot be disputed' - so it is read as asserted rather than supposed."
    }

[A severe struggle for life]: Owing to the high geometrical powers of increase of each species, there is at some age, season or year a severe struggle for life.
    {
    fidelity: "paraphrase"
    source: "\"there be, owing to the high geometrical powers of increase of each species, at some age, season or year, a severe struggle for life\""
    note: "Darwin's clause is reordered and his 'there be' made assertoric, so the claim text is a paraphrase however exact the span beside it. He endorses this antecedent too - 'and this certainly cannot be disputed'. The second paragraph re-describes the same struggle as 'the incessant struggle of all species to increase in numbers', which is why one claim serves both halves of the passage."
    }

[Diversity is advantageous]: The infinite complexity of the relations of all organic beings to each other and to their conditions of existence causes an infinite diversity in structure, constitution, and habits to be advantageous to them.
    {
    fidelity: "paraphrase"
    source: "\"the infinite complexity of the relations of all organic beings to each other and to their conditions of existence, causing an infinite diversity in structure, constitution, and habits, to be advantageous to them\""
    note: "Darwin's participle 'causing' is made a finite verb so that the premise stands as a statement; nothing else is altered."
    }

[Variations useful to man have occurred]: So many variations have occurred useful to man.
    {
    fidelity: "quotation"
    source: "\"so many variations have occurred useful to man\""
    note: "Attached in the source by 'in the same way as'. The domestic case is what makes the absence of useful variation extraordinary, so it is a premise of the argument and not an aside."
    }

[It would be extraordinary if no useful variation had occurred]: I think it would be a most extraordinary fact if no variation ever had occurred useful to each being's own welfare.
    {
    fidelity: "quotation"
    source: "\"I think it would be a most extraordinary fact if no variation ever had occurred useful to each being's own welfare\""
    note: "Darwin's 'I think' is kept inside the claim rather than trimmed to tidy it. The conclusion is his judgement about what would be extraordinary, not a demonstration that useful variations occur, and cutting the hedge would report a stronger claim than he makes."
    }

[Useful variations occur]: Variations useful to a being's own welfare do occur.
    {
    fidelity: "interpretation"
    warrant: "enthymeme"
    source: "\"variations useful to any organic being do occur\""
    note: "Darwin never asserts this. He argues that its falsehood would be extraordinary, and then reasons from the antecedent of a further conditional - 'But if variations useful to any organic being do occur'. Detaching that antecedent is the reconstruction's step, and everything downstream of it rests on the step."
    }

[The favoured are preserved]: Individuals characterised by useful variations will assuredly have the best chance of being preserved in the struggle for life.
    {
    fidelity: "paraphrase"
    source: "\"assuredly individuals thus characterised will have the best chance of being preserved in the struggle for life\""
    note: "'thus characterised' is resolved to 'characterised by useful variations'. The scope is Darwin's: the best chance, not certainty."
    }

[The principle of inheritance is strong]: The principle of inheritance is strong.
    {
    fidelity: "paraphrase"
    source: "\"from the strong principle of inheritance\""
    note: "Darwin appeals to this rather than arguing for it; in his sentence it is a definite description, and it is made assertoric here so that it can serve as a premise."
    }

[The favoured leave offspring like themselves]: Individuals characterised by useful variations will tend to produce offspring similarly characterised.
    {
    fidelity: "paraphrase"
    source: "\"they will tend to produce offspring similarly characterised\""
    note: "The pronoun is resolved; Darwin's hedge 'tend to' is kept."
    }

[Diversification improves the chance of success]: The more diversified the descendants of any one species become, the better will be their chance of succeeding in the battle of life.
    {
    fidelity: "paraphrase"
    source: "\"the more diversified these descendants become, the better will be their chance of succeeding in the battle of life\""
    note: "'these descendants' is resolved from the same sentence's 'the descendants of any one species'."
    }

[Varietal differences grow into specific differences]: The small differences distinguishing varieties of the same species, will steadily tend to increase till they come to equal the greater differences between species of the same genus, or even of distinct genera.
    {
    fidelity: "quotation"
    source: "\"the small differences distinguishing varieties of the same species, will steadily tend to increase till they come to equal the greater differences between species of the same genus, or even of distinct genera\""
    note: "A tendency claim, and it is left one: differences 'steadily tend to increase', and they increase 'till they come to equal' specific and even generic differences. Darwin does not say that every variety becomes a species."
    }

[The principle of preservation is called natural selection]: This principle of preservation, I have called, for the sake of brevity, Natural Selection.
    {
    fidelity: "quotation"
    source: "\"This principle of preservation, I have called, for the sake of brevity, Natural Selection\""
    note: "A naming rather than a further inference: the demonstrative reaches back to the preservation argument, across an ellipsis. It sits at the apex because it is where the passage stops and nothing in the passage rests on it. The pinpoint p. 168 is the author's and is unverified."
    }

